.TH SH-DYNSYS 1
.SH NAME
dynsys \- shell script interface to dynsys(2)'s ODE-system language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load dynsys

.B id := ${dynsys new}
.br
.B dynsys
.I id
.I verb args...
.br
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B check}
.br
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B initialstate}
.br
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B rhs
.I t v0 v1 ...
.B }
.br
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B observables
.I v0 v1 ...
.B }
.br
.B dynsys del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dynsys
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own ODE-system language, the same relationship
.IR sh-pde (1)
already has to
.IR pde (2):
a handle table around the library's five entry points, adding no
vocabulary of its own.
.PP
Unlike
.IR sh-pde (1)
(which accumulates spec TEXT lines and defers parsing to one later
.BR commit ),
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own
.B cmd()
already executes each line immediately \(em see
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own description ("one line in, effect immediate ... never staged for a
later pass"). So there is no
.B commit
verb here either: every word after the handle id is forwarded
.I UNSEEN
straight to
.BR dynsys->cmd() ,
the same discipline
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)'s
own
.B cmd()
holds to \(em this file does not recognize
.BR param / maxtime / state / derivative / eq / observable
itself; see
.IR dynsys (2)
for that vocabulary in full.
.PP
.B ${dynsys new}
allocates a handle and its own fresh
.IR dynsys (2)
model (and, transitively, its own fresh
.IR hoc (2)
instance \(em see
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own BUGS).
.B Dynsys
.I id verb args...
passes
.IR verb " " args...
as one already-tokenized line to
.BR dynsys->cmd() ,
raising an error if it comes back malformed or unclaimed.
.PP
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.BR check }
calls
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own
.BR check() ,
yielding
.B ""
if the model is ready to run, or an error string naming what's
missing (call once after every line of a script has run, the same
place
.IR dynsys (2)'s
own man page puts it).
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.BR initialstate }
yields one word per state variable, in declaration order.
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B rhs
.I t v0 v1
.BR ... }
binds
.I t
and the given state values and yields one word per derivative, in the
same order \(em the number of
.I v
arguments must equal the model's own number of states.
.B ${dynsys
.I id
.B observables
.I v0 v1
.BR ... }
does the same binding and yields one word per
.B observable
line, in declaration order.
.B Dynsys del
frees one or more handles.
.SH EXAMPLE
A decaying quantity,
.IR x ,
with rate constant
.IR k ,
stepped by hand from the shell (a real integration loop would call
.B rhs
repeatedly through
.IR numerics (2)'s
own
.BR euler ()/ rk4 (),
the same way
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
does in Limbo):
.EX
	load dynsys
	id := ${dynsys new}
	dynsys $id param k 1.0 0.0 10.0
	dynsys $id maxtime 10.0
	dynsys $id state x 1.0
	dynsys $id derivative x '- k x'
	dynsys $id observable x2 'x x'
	echo check: ${dynsys $id check}
	echo initial: ${dynsys $id initialstate}
	echo derivative at t=0, x=1: ${dynsys $id rhs 0.0 1.0}
	echo observables at x=2: ${dynsys $id observables 2.0}
	dynsys del $id
.EE
Every expression above is
.IR eqn (7)
syntax (see
.IR eqn2hoc (2)):
multiplication is juxtaposition
.RI ( "k x" ,
not
.IR "k*x" ),
and a leading unary minus needs a space
.RI ( "- k x" ,
not
.IR "-k*x" ,
which
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
does not accept).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/dynsys.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-pde (1),
.IR sh-hoc (1),
.IR sh-mesh (1),
.IR sh-fem (1),
.IR danby-aerobraking (1)
.SH BUGS
Every argument to
.BR rhs / observables
is a plain shell word, so passing the wrong count silently becomes a
usage error naming the expected count \(em there is no way to bind
values by name from the shell, only by the model's own declaration
order.
